<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=siege-tower">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege tower</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as siege towers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A siege tower is a mobile wooden tower used to bring attackers to the top of fortification walls. It was used from ancient times across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> note that Assyrian engineers developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battering-ram">battering rams</a>, siege towers and undermining techniques to capture fortified cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-tower.jpg" alt="Siege tower" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roque Gameiro · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conquista_de_Lisboa_(Roque_Gameiro,_Quadros_da_Hist%C3%B3ria_de_Portugal,_1917).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A siege tower, called a belfry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, is a specialised siege engine built to protect assailants and ladders as they approach the walls of a fortification. It was often rectangular, with four wheels, and as high as the wall or higher, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> or crossbowmen on top could shoot into the fortification. The towers were mostly wooden and so flammable, and builders covered them with iron or fresh animal skins. Because of their size they were mostly built on the site of the siege, which took considerable time, and they were used mainly when ladders, mining or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battering-ram">battering rams</a> could not overcome the defences.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>When a tower reached the wall, it dropped a gangplank across, and troops rushed onto the walls. Some towers also carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battering-ram">battering rams</a> or housed spearmen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a>, swordsmen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and crossbowmen. Being large, a tower was often the first target of stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a>, but it had projectiles of its own. A tower could be defeated by flooding the ground before the wall so that it bogged down in mud, and it needed level ground, so many hill-top forts were safe from it.</p><p>Small towers were sometimes placed on earth mounds, and the Roman siege ramp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a> in 72 to 73 CE survives. Towers were also mounted on ships to attack coastal walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Evidence for siege towers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. A mobile tower appears in the tomb of General Intef at Thebes, and a stone relief of about 2000 BCE from Harpoot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> is the earliest known depiction from Anatolia. The Neo-Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> used them in the 9th century BCE. Piye, founder of the Kushite 25th dynasty, had one built at Ashmunein. The Helepolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius I of Macedon</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 305 BCE stood up to 40 metres high, had nine storeys and was manned by 200 soldiers.</p><p>Use reached its height in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Avar attackers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 626 prepared twelve towers covered with hides, and at Kenilworth in 1266 a single tower carried 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and 11 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a>. Defenders sprayed Ottoman towers with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a> in 1453. Large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> made towers obsolete, though wooden battery towers, such as one built by Ivan Vyrodkov at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in 1552, took on a similar role.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege tower” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Engineers used wheeled battering rams, <b>siege towers</b>, ramps and mining to reduce heavily fortified cities, as the reliefs of Sennacherib's siege of Lachish in 701 BCE record.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 397 BCE Dionysius I of Syracuse besieged Motya, building a mole across the lagoon and using <b>siege towers</b> and catapults; the city fell after fierce street fighting.</blockquote><a href="/state/motya">Motya</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 2000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Harpoot, Turkey</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>A mobile siege tower is shown in the First Intermediate Period tomb of General Intef at Thebes, Egypt, which is not dated more closely.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=siege-tower">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
